Historical records remain useful only while consumers can translate their versions. A deployment can break recovery without changing newly produced data.

> Schema evolution ensures that the consumer can translate it to a familiar version.

Source: [Q12](/lore/durability-bibliography#q12); [B5](/lore/durability-bibliography#b5-building-event-driven-microservices), [B14](/lore/durability-bibliography#b14-refactoring-databases).

## Failure it prevents

A new consumer cannot decode already-accepted work.

## Our Effect 4.0.0 reading

`Schema.decodeUnknownEffect` validates a representation. Define supported versions and transformations; the decoder does not choose the compatibility policy.

Source: `packages/effect/src/Schema.ts`, `packages/effect/src/persistence/PersistedQueue.ts`.

## How to check

Keep old payload fixtures. Decode and restore them with the proposed consumer before removing old representations. This check is our proposal. The books do not prescribe it.

Continue with [Shared schemas need a transition period](/lore/schema-changes-need-a-transition), [Replay needs stable inputs](/lore/replay-needs-stable-inputs).

Connect this to [the existing rat-stack guidance](/lore/schemas-define-the-boundary).

